  "configuration_id": "config_021",
  "reason": "Evidence: We have only one completed experiment (config_002: rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) at ~48.5% accuracy, giving us a weak baseline with no information about whether multiprofile representations add signal. Question: Does moving from single to multiple profiles (with the simplest aggregation, avgpool) improve performance while keeping the same PCA encoder? This isolates the profile/aggregation structural decision without confounding it with encoder or modality changes. Why this is the best next experiment: Multiprofile is a fundamental design axis that governs a large fraction of the remaining search space. Testing it with the simplest aggregation (avgpool) and the already-established PCA encoder gives a clean, low-risk comparison to the baseline. If multiprofile+avgpool substantially improves accuracy, it validates the multiprofile direction and justifies investing further in aggregation and fusion choices; if it does not, we can deprioritize many multiprofile configurations and focus budget on encoder or modality questions instead."
}

